# Renders GitHub's native "Cite this repository" button in the sidebar, which
# emits APA and BibTeX matching what cloro.dev/ai-visibility/ shows on the page.
# Both must agree: someone citing from the site and someone citing from here
# should produce the same reference.
#
# This describes the dataset as a whole. To cite one week specifically, keep the
# reference below and point the URL at that week's file under snapshots/ — the
# weekly file is the thing that doesn't change.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: AI Visibility Leaderboard
message: >-
  If you use this data, please cite it as below and link either this repository
  or https://cloro.dev/ai-visibility/.
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: cloro
    website: "https://cloro.dev"
repository-code: "https://github.com/cloro-dev/ai-visibility-leaderboard"
url: "https://cloro.dev/ai-visibility/"
abstract: >-
  A weekly measurement of which brands AI assistants actually recommend. Every
  Monday, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, Copilot and Google AI Mode are asked the
  same buyer-intent questions across twelve software categories, and the answers
  are scored for which brands they mention and which they cite. Each week is
  published as a dated snapshot, so a citation resolves to the data as it stood
  rather than to a page that has since moved on. Detection is deterministic
  string and URL matching, not an LLM judgement, so the same answers always
  produce the same score.
keywords:
  - ai visibility
  - ai search
  - generative engine optimization
  - llm
  - brand monitoring
  - chatgpt
  - perplexity
  - dataset
license: CC-BY-4.0
date-released: "2026-07-27"
